+# Agent Note: Additive workspace extension slots
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-09-09-workspace-extension-slots.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+An external feature needs a workspace-header action and workspace-specific menu items. Occupying `sidebar.workspaces` to add these entries replaces native search, ordering and directory picking, coupling a small feature to private browser implementation and complicating uninstallation.
+
+## Decision
+
+The existing browser declares `sidebar.workspaces.header.action` and `sidebar.workspaces.menu.action` as root-scoped list slots. The header has no owner share; the menu passes only a real Workspace id and a close callback. The browser authorizes its nested row to render the menu slot. The shared Menu primitive accepts an optional `beforeItems` region within its existing scrolling and dismissal boundary. Business logic remains with contributors.
+
+Contributors use declaration-lifetime `slots.inject` registration. Ordering, unloading and owner re-declaration use the existing runtime. Menu contributors supply accessible controls and close the native menu before taking action. Header contributions appear only in the wide sidebar with collapsed search; rail affordances can use existing sidebar slots.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+Copying or replacing WorkspaceBrowser duplicates unrelated behavior and tracks private source. DOM injection bypasses lifecycle and accessibility contracts. A separate menu-action registry duplicates the slot runtime. A generic primitive region keeps the new slot contract with the workspace owner and the Menu primitive independent of business domains.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Plugins can add and remove actions while the native browser remains mounted. Consumers require a host build declaring these slots; older builds cannot render them. Contributors own their menu-item semantics and styling. Slots do not grant execution permission; each feature's host service remains responsible for authorization.
+
+The extension assembly tests cover empty native menus, multiple ordered contributors, identity across two Workspaces, closing before actions, removal while a menu is open, retained native Rename, registration before owner availability and owner remount without duplicates. Existing workspace tests retain search, ordering and directory-picker coverage.
+
+Full browser verification exposed two snapshot-readiness races: the background-job count can settle before its independently subscribed open list, and locale can resolve before the settings preset inventory. Those tests now wait for the list's SIGTERM outcome and the enabled preset selector respectively; their expected snapshots and product behavior stay unchanged.
+
+The built-boot smoke reads the recorded client build profile when asserting branding. A local build must retain its local identity; requiring the official wordmark would reject the supported default build before exercising the workspace journey.
